course: dictionary
lesson: 6886
title: Words for things we wear
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name things you wear that you may never have seen before. The other words in the sentence often give you clues. «She pulled on her overshoes before she stepped into the mud.» Pulled on and mud tell you that overshoes go on your feet and are for dirty ground.
explain: Check your guess by swapping your meaning into the sentence. An overshoe is a shoe you wear over your other shoe, so «She pulled on her overshoes before she stepped into the mud» still makes sense.
explain: Words from other times and places can be different. «Goloshes» and «galoshes» are older names for overshoes, so an old story may use them. When a word is still new to you, read on for clues, and use a dictionary if you are not sure.
fact: An overshoe is a rubber or plastic shoe worn over an ordinary shoe.
fact: Overshoes keep your shoes dry or clean.
fact: «Goloshes» are rubber overshoes for mud and snow, and «galoshes» is another spelling of the same word.
fact: In older stories and in cold places such as Canada, people wore overshoes in snow and mud.
fact: Today hospitals and building sites use plastic overshoes to keep floors clean.
exercise: «His goloshes, which always lay by the umbrella-stand, were also gone.» What are goloshes? | Hats worn in the rain | *Rubber overshoes for mud and snow | Umbrellas that fold up small
exercise: What is the main job of an overshoe? | To make your feet run faster | To hold an umbrella up | *To keep your shoes dry or clean
exercise: Which place uses plastic overshoes today? | *A hospital | A hot desert | A sunny beach
test: What is an overshoe? | *A rubber or plastic shoe worn over an ordinary shoe | A shoe worn for dancing | A tall boot that reaches the knee
test: «Goloshes» were worn in older stories and cold places. Where were they worn? | On a hot beach in summer | In a swimming pool | *In snow and mud
exam: Why would someone wear overshoes on a snowy day? | To make their feet look bigger | *To keep their shoes dry and clean | To keep their hands warm
